          "title": "Character Codes (ASCII, Unicode) & Data in Memory",
          "markdown": "## 3. Character Codes (ASCII, Unicode) & Data in Memory\n\n* **ASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange):**\n  * *Definition & Working:* Standard **7-bit** character encoding ($2^7 = 128$ codes: `0–31` non-printable control like `Enter/Tab`, `32` Space, `'0'–'9' = 48–57`, `'A'–'Z' = 65–90`, `'a'–'z' = 97–122`; Extended ASCII uses 8 bits).\n  * *Advantage & Disadvantage:* Ultra-compact (1 byte fits cleanly in RAM), universal for English; fails completely for Hindi, Arabic, Chinese, math symbols, or emojis!\n* **Unicode (`UTF-8 / UTF-16 / UTF-32`):**\n  * *Definition & Working:* Universal global character set supporting **1,114,112 code points (`U+0000` to `U+10FFFF`)** covering every world script (**Devanagari `अ` = `U+0905`**), mathematical operators, and emojis. **UTF-8** uses variable-width $1$–$4$ bytes while keeping the first $128$ codes **100% backward-compatible with ASCII**!\n* **Data Representation in Memory:** Computer RAM is **byte-addressable** ($1\\text{ Byte} = 8\\text{ bits}$; $1\\text{ KB} = 1024\\text{ B}$ or $2^{10}$, $1\\text{ MB} = 2^{20}\\text{ B}$, $1\\text{ GB} = 2^{30}\\text{ B}$). Multi-byte integers/floats (`IEEE-754` 32-bit float: 1 sign + 8 exponent + 23 mantissa bits) sit across consecutive byte cells (`Little-Endian` on x86 puts LSB at lowest RAM address)."
