          "markdown": "## 4. Sixteen Solved Unit 2 Problems (Step-by-Step Solutions)\n\n### Problem 1 (BEU 2026 Paper Q.5 — Decimal to Binary, Octal & Hexadecimal)\nConvert **$(156)_{10}$** into binary, octal, and hexadecimal.\n1. Repeatedly divide $156$ by $2$:\n   $156/2 = 78\\text{ R }0$, $78/2 = 39\\text{ R }0$, $39/2 = 19\\text{ R }1$, $19/2 = 9\\text{ R }1$, $9/2 = 4\\text{ R }1$, $4/2 = 2\\text{ R }0$, $2/2 = 1\\text{ R }0$, $1/2 = 0\\text{ R }1$.\n   Read bottom-to-top $\\implies \\mathbf{(10011100)_2}$.\n2. Group in 3 bits for Octal: $\\underbrace{010}_2\\,\\underbrace{011}_3\\,\\underbrace{100}_4 \\implies \\mathbf{(234)_8}$.\n3. Group in 4 bits for Hex: $\\underbrace{1001}_9\\,\\underbrace{1100}_{12=\\text{C}} \\implies \\mathbf{(9\\text{C})_{16}}$.\n\n### Problem 2 (Fractional Decimal to Binary & Hexadecimal)\nConvert **$(43.625)_{10}$** to binary and hexadecimal.\n1. Integer $43$: $32+8+2+1 \\implies 101011_2$.\n2. Fraction $0.625 \\times 2 = \\mathbf{1}.25$; $0.25 \\times 2 = \\mathbf{0}.50$; $0.50 \\times 2 = \\mathbf{1}.00 \\implies .101_2$.\n3. Combined Binary = **$(101011.101)_2$**.\n4. Group 4s outward from point: $0010\\,1011\\,.\\,1010_2 = \\mathbf{(2\\text{B.A})_{16}}$.\n\n### Problem 3 (Fractional Binary to Decimal, Octal & Hexadecimal)\nConvert **$(1101101.101)_2$** to decimal, octal, and hex.\n1. Decimal weights: $2^6+2^5+2^3+2^2+2^0 + 2^{-1}+2^{-3} = 64+32+8+4+1+0.5+0.125 = \\mathbf{109.625_{10}}$.\n2. Group in 3s: $\\underbrace{001}_1\\,\\underbrace{101}_5\\,\\underbrace{101}_5\\,.\\,\\underbrace{101}_5 = \\mathbf{(155.5)_8}$.\n3. Group in 4s: $\\underbrace{0110}_6\\,\\underbrace{1101}_{13=\\text{D}}\\,.\\,\\underbrace{1010}_{10=\\text{A}} = \\mathbf{(6\\text{D.A})_{16}}$.\n\n### Problem 4 (Fractional Octal to Binary & Decimal)\nConvert **$(736.4)_8$** to binary and decimal.\n1. Expand each octal digit into 3 bits: $7=111, 3=011, 6=110, .4=100 \\implies \\mathbf{(111011110.1)_2}$.\n2. Decimal: $7(8^2) + 3(8^1) + 6(8^0) + 4(8^{-1}) = 448 + 24 + 6 + 0.5 = \\mathbf{478.5_{10}}$.\n\n### Problem 5 (Fractional Hexadecimal to Binary, Octal & Decimal)\nConvert **$(2\\text{F9.C})_{16}$** to binary, octal, and decimal.\n1. Expand 4 bits per hex digit: $2=0010, \\text{F}=1111, 9=1001, .\\text{C}=1100 \\implies \\mathbf{(1011111001.11)_2}$.\n2. Regroup in 3s outward: $001\\,011\\,111\\,001\\,.\\,110_2 = \\mathbf{(1371.6)_8}$.\n3. Decimal: $2(256) + 15(16) + 9 + 12/16 = 512 + 240 + 9 + 0.75 = \\mathbf{761.75_{10}}$.\n\n### Problem 6 (Non-Terminating Binary Fraction Precision)\nConvert **$(19.6)_{10}$** to binary up to 5 fractional bits.\n1. Integer $19 = 16 + 2 + 1 = 10011_2$.\n2. Fraction $0.6 \\times 2$: $\\mathbf{1}.2 \\to \\mathbf{0}.4 \\to \\mathbf{0}.8 \\to \\mathbf{1}.6 \\to \\mathbf{1}.2 \\dots$ (repeating cycle `1001`!).\n3. Result (5 fractional bits) = **$(10011.10011)_2$**.\n\n### Problem 7 (Binary Integer Addition)\nCompute **$(101101)_2 + (11011)_2$** ($45 + 27$).\n```text\n  Carry:  1 1 1 1 1 1\n            1 0 1 1 0 1   (45)\n          + 0 1 1 0 1 1   (27)\n          -------------\n          1 0 0 1 0 0 0   (72)\n```\n**Answer:** **$(1001000)_2$** ($= 64 + 8 = 72_{10}$ $\\checkmark$).\n\n### Problem 8 (Binary Fractional Addition)\nCompute **$(1011.101)_2 + (110.11)_2$** ($11.625 + 6.75$).\n```text\n            1 0 1 1. 1 0 1   (11.625)\n          + 0 1 1 0. 1 1 0   ( 6.750)\n          -----------------\n          1 0 0 1 0. 0 1 1   (18.375)\n```\n**Answer:** **$(10010.011)_2$** ($18.375_{10}$ $\\checkmark$).\n\n### Problem 9 (Direct Binary Subtraction via Borrow)\nCompute **$(110101)_2 - (10111)_2$** ($53 - 23$).\n```text\n            1 1 0 1 0 1   (53)\n          - 0 1 0 1 1 1   (23)\n          -------------\n            0 1 1 1 1 0   (30)\n```\n*(Column $2^0$: $1-1=0$; $2^1$: borrow from $2^2$, $2-1=1$; $2^2$: $0$ borrows from $2^4\\dots \\to 011110_2 = 16+8+4+2 = 30_{10}$.)*\n\n### Problem 10 (6-Bit 1's Complement Subtraction — Positive Result)\nSubtract **$19$ from $29$** ($29 - 19 = +10$) using **6-bit 1's complement**.\n1. $+29 = 011101_2$; $+19 = 010011_2 \\implies$ 1's comp of $19 = 101100_2$.\n2. Add: $011101 + 101100 = 1\\,001001_2$ (7th bit = end-around carry **`1`**!).\n3. Add end-around carry `1` to `001001`: $001001 + 1 = \\mathbf{001010_2}$ ($+10_{10}$ $\\checkmark$).\n\n### Problem 11 (6-Bit 1's Complement Subtraction — Negative Result)\nSubtract **$23$ from $14$** ($14 - 23 = -9$) using **6-bit 1's complement**.\n1. $+14 = 001110_2$; $+23 = 010111_2 \\implies$ 1's comp of $23 = 101000_2$.\n2. Add: $001110 + 101000 = \\mathbf{110110_2}$ (**No carry-out** $\\implies$ Negative!).\n3. Invert `110110` to verify magnitude: $001001_2 = 9 \\implies \\mathbf{-(001001)_2 = -9_{10}}$ $\\checkmark$.\n\n### Problem 12 (8-Bit 2's Complement Subtraction — Positive Result)\nCompute **$54 - 28$** using **8-bit 2's complement**.\n1. $+54 = 00110110_2$; $+28 = 00011100_2$.\n2. 2's comp of $28$: `11100011 + 1 = 11100100_2` ($-28$).\n3. Add: $00110110 + 11100100 = 1\\,00011010_2$.\n4. Discard 9th carry bit $\\implies$ **$\\mathbf{00011010_2} = +26_{10}$** $\\checkmark$.\n\n### Problem 13 (8-Bit 2's Complement Subtraction — Negative Result)\nCompute **$18 - 45$** using **8-bit 2's complement**.\n1. $+18 = 00010010_2$; $+45 = 00101101_2 \\implies$ 2's comp of $45 = 11010011_2$ ($-45$).\n2. Add: $00010010 + 11010011 = \\mathbf{11100101_2}$ (No carry out; MSB = `1` $\\implies$ negative in 2's complement!).\n3. Check value: take 2's comp of `11100101` = `00011010 + 1 = 00011011_2 = 27` $\\implies \\mathbf{-27_{10}}$ $\\checkmark$.\n\n### Problem 14 (All Three 8-Bit Signed Formats for $-37$)\nRepresent **$-37$** in 8-bit Sign-Magnitude, 1's Complement, and 2's Complement.\n* $+37 = 00100101_2$.\n* **Sign-Magnitude:** Set MSB to `1` $\\implies \\mathbf{10100101_2}$\n* **1's Complement:** Flip all bits $\\implies \\mathbf{11011010_2}$\n* **2's Complement:** Add `1` to 1's comp $\\implies \\mathbf{11011011_2}$\n\n### Problem 15 (4-Bit Signed Overflow Detection + Unknown Base BEU Puzzle)\n1. **4-Bit 2's Complement Overflow:** Add $(+5) + (+6)$ in 4-bit 2's comp (`range: -8 to +7`).\n   $0101 + 0110 = \\mathbf{1011_2}$. Look! Both inputs were positive (`MSB=0`), yet output has `MSB=1` (meaning **$-5$** in signed 4-bit!). **SIGNED OVERFLOW OCCURRED** because $+11 > +7$.\n2. **BEU 2026 Unknown Base Problem:** Solve $(123)_5 = (x8)_y$.\n   * LHS $= 1(25)+2(5)+3 = 38_{10}$. RHS $= xy + 8$. Thus $xy = 30$.\n   * Since digit `8` appears, $y \\ge 9$, and $1 \\le x < y$. Factor pairs of $30$ with $y \\ge 9$: **$(3, 10)$, $(2, 15)$, and $(1, 30)$** (**3 solutions**!).\n\n### Problem 16 (ASCII Encoding & Memory Sizing)\n1. Encode string **`\"AI-1\"`** in ASCII (Decimal, Hex, 8-bit Binary):\n   * `'A' = 65 = 0x41 = 01000001`, `'I' = 73 = 0x49 = 01001001`, `'-' = 45 = 0x2D = 00101101`, `'1' = 49 = 0x31 = 00110001`.\n2. **Memory Sizing:** How many Kilobytes (`KB`, binary $1024\\text{ B}$) do $1024$ 32-bit integers consume, and how many **bits** sit inside an $8\\text{ GiB}$ RAM stick?\n   * $1024 \\times 4\\text{ Bytes} = 4096\\text{ B} = \\mathbf{4\\text{ KB}}$.\n   * $8 \\times 1024^3 \\text{ B} \\times 8\\text{ bits/B} = \\mathbf{68{,}719{,}476{,}736\\text{ bits}}$ ($2^{36}$ bits)."
