          "markdown": "## 5. Full Exam Question Bank — Unit 2 (With Model Answers)\n\n### Part A: 10 Two-Mark Questions\n\n1. **Why do digital computers prefer Binary over Decimal internally?**\n   *Model Answer:* Two voltage states (`ON`/`OFF`) give high electrical noise tolerance, cheap bi-stable transistor design, and simple Boolean arithmetic circuits compared to maintaining 10 voltage levels.\n2. **Define Bit, Nibble, Byte, and Word.**\n   *Model Answer:* `Bit` = single `0/1`; `Nibble` = `4 bits` (1 Hex digit); `Byte` = `8 bits`; `Word` = native data width processed at once by a CPU (`32 bits` or `64 bits`).\n3. **Give the signed range formula for $n$-bit 1's Complement vs. 2's Complement.**\n   *Model Answer:* 1's Complement covers $[-(2^{n-1}-1),\\; +(2^{n-1}-1)]$ (symmetric, two zeros); 2's Complement covers $[-2^{n-1},\\; +(2^{n-1}-1)]$ (one unique zero, e.g., $-128$ to $+127$ for $n=8$).\n4. **How many unique characters can standard 7-bit ASCII represent?**\n   *Model Answer:* $2^7 = \\mathbf{128}$ characters (`0` to `127`); Extended ASCII adds a parity/8th bit for $256$ codes.\n5. **Convert `(0100 1111. 1100)_2` to Octal (`BEU 2026`).**\n   *Model Answer:* Regroup by 3s around point: `001 | 001 | 111. 110` $\\implies \\mathbf{(117.6)_8}$.\n6. **Find the 8-bit 2's complement of `-25` (`BEU 2026`).**\n   *Model Answer:* $+25 = 00011001_2 \\to$ invert $= 11100110_2 \\to$ add $1 \\implies \\mathbf{11100111_2}$.\n7. **State the rule for Signed Overflow in 2's complement addition.**\n   *Model Answer:* Overflow occurs **only** when adding two numbers of the *same sign* produces a result with the *opposite sign bit* (equivalently, carry-in $\\ne$ carry-out of the MSB).\n8. **Write the ASCII decimal and hex codes for `'0'`, `'A'`, and `'a'`.**\n   *Model Answer:* `'0' = 48 (0x30)`, `'A' = 65 (0x41)`, `'a' = 97 (0x61)` (notice lowercase = uppercase $+ 32$).\n9. **What is the key advantage of UTF-8 over ASCII and UTF-32?**\n   *Model Answer:* UTF-8 stores English ASCII in just **1 byte** (zero bloat, 100% ASCII-compatible) while seamlessly expanding up to **4 bytes** to encode every global language and emoji, unlike fixed 4-byte UTF-32.\n10. **Convert `(2B.A)_16` to Binary directly.**\n    *Model Answer:* `2 = 0010`, `B = 1011`, `.A = .1010` $\\implies \\mathbf{(101011.101)_2}$.\n\n---\n\n### Part B: 6 Five-Mark Questions\n\n1. **Explain Sign-Magnitude, 1's Complement, and 2's Complement signed formats using $-37$ (8-bit) as an example, comparing zero representation and range.**\n   *Model Answer:* Show `+37 = 00100101`, `SM = 10100101`, `1's = 11011010`, `2's = 11011011` (Problem 14). Highlight Sign-Magnitude & 1's Comp suffer dual zeros (`+0` and `-0`) and range $[-127, +127]$, whereas 2's Comp eliminates `-0`, covers $[-128, +127]$, and lets one Adder handle subtraction cleanly.\n\n2. **Subtract `(18)_10` from `(43)_10` (`43 - 18`) AND `(45)_10` from `(18)_10` (`18 - 45`) using 8-bit 2's complement arithmetic.**\n   *Model Answer:* Show $+43 = 00101011$, $-18 = 11101110 \\implies \\text{sum } = 1\\,00011001 \\to \\text{drop carry} \\to \\mathbf{00011001_2 (+25)}$. Then show $+18 = 00010010$, $-45 = 11010011 \\implies \\mathbf{11100101_2}$ (no carry, MSB=1 $\\to -27$).\n\n3. **Convert `(2F9.C)_16` to Binary, Octal, and Decimal showing every step.**\n   *Model Answer:* Work Problem 5 step-by-step: hex nibbles $\\to 1011111001.11_2$, 3-bit clustering $\\to (1371.6)_8$, positional weights $\\to 761.75_{10}$.\n\n4. **Solve `(123)_5 = (x8)_y` finding all valid base-digit pairs `(x, y)` (`BEU 2026`).**\n   *Model Answer:* Work Problem 15 part 2: $38 = xy + 8 \\implies xy = 30$; require $y \\ge 9$ (because digit `8` exists) and $1 \\le x < y$, giving exact 3 valid solutions: **`(3, 10)`, `(2, 15)`, and `(1, 30)`**.\n\n5. **Compare 7-bit ASCII vs. Unicode (`UTF-8`, `UTF-16`, `UTF-32`) in detail, encoding `\"AI-1\"` in binary/hex.**\n   *Model Answer:* Contrast 128 English-only ASCII codes vs. 1.1M+ universal Unicode glyphs (`UTF-8` 1–4 byte variable vs. `UTF-32` fixed 4-byte). Show `'A'(0x41), 'I'(0x49), '-'(0x2D), '1'(0x31)` from Problem 16.\n\n6. **Perform `(1011.101)_2 + (110.11)_2` and compute `29 - 19` plus `14 - 23` using 6-bit 1's complement arithmetic.**\n   *Model Answer:* Work Problems 8, 10 (`011101 + 101100 = 1_001001 -> +1 -> 001010 = +10`), and 11 (`001110 + 101000 = 110110 -> -9`).\n\n---\n\n### Part C: 4 Ten-Mark Questions\n\n1. **Explain Positional Number Systems thoroughly (`Binary, Octal, Decimal, Hexadecimal`) and execute full step-by-step conversions: (a) `(156)_10` to Bin/Oct/Hex, (b) `(43.625)_10` to Bin/Hex, (c) `(736.4)_8` to Bin/Dec, and (d) `(1101101.101)_2` to Dec/Oct/Hex.**\n   *Model Answer:* Present Section 1 theory + table + complete verified solutions from Problems 1, 2, 3, and 4 (`10011100_2 / 234_8 / 9C_16`, `101011.101_2 / 2B.A_16`, `111011110.1_2 / 478.5_10`, `109.625_10 / 155.5_8 / 6D.A_16`).\n\n2. **Compare Unsigned, Sign-Magnitude, 1's Complement, and 2's Complement arithmetic inside an ALU. Demonstrate addition, subtraction (both positive and negative outcomes), and 4-bit Signed Overflow (`+5 + +6`) with complete binary carries.**\n   *Model Answer:* Present Section 2 explanations, range/zero comparison, and step-by-step binary traces from Problems 10, 12, 13, and 15 showing both end-around carry (1's comp) vs. discarded carry (2's comp) and overflow check.\n\n3. **Explain how Text, Positive/Negative Integers, and Real Numbers (`IEEE-754`) are stored inside byte-addressable RAM. Compute storage sizes for arrays, bit counts for `8 GiB`, and explain why ASCII needed replacement by Unicode UTF-8.**\n   *Model Answer:* Cover byte addressability, KB/MB/GB binary powers ($2^{10}, 2^{20}, 2^{30}$), Little-Endian storage, IEEE-754 Sign/Exponent/Mantissa layout, Section 3 ASCII vs. Unicode comparison, and Problem 16 calculations (`4 KB`, `68,719,476,736 bits`).\n\n4. **Solve this Mixed Data-Representation Numerical Marathon: (a) `(19.6)_10` to binary (5 frac bits), (b) `(101101)_2 + (11011)_2`, (c) `(110101)_2 - (10111)_2`, (d) Represent `-37` in SM, 1's, and 2's complement (8-bit), and (e) Compute `18 - 45` via 8-bit 2's complement.**\n   *Model Answer:* Combine the complete step-by-step solutions from Problems 6, 7, 9, 14, and 13 cleanly with all carries, borrows, and sign checks clearly boxed."
