          "markdown": "## 5. Full Exam Question Bank — Unit 3 (With Model Answers)\n\n### Part A: 10 Two-Mark Questions\n\n1. **Differentiate between a Program and a Process.**\n   *Model Answer:* A *Program* is a static, passive executable file stored on disk (`main.exe`). A *Process* is an active, dynamic instance of a running program occupying RAM, registers, Program Counter, and PCB.\n2. **State the two primary objectives of an Operating System.**\n   *Model Answer:* (1) **Convenience** — making hardware user-friendly and intuitive; (2) **Efficiency** — scheduling CPU, RAM, and I/O fairly for maximum throughput.\n3. **What is a Device Driver?**\n   *Model Answer:* Special system software enabling the generic OS kernel to communicate cleanly with specific hardware models (printers, GPUs, Wi-Fi cards).\n4. **Distinguish Hard Real-Time OS from Soft Real-Time OS.**\n   *Model Answer:* Missing a deadline in a *Hard RTOS* triggers total mission failure (e.g., airbag/pacemaker), whereas in a *Soft RTOS* missed deadlines slightly degrade quality without catastrophe (e.g., live video frames).\n5. **What is Context Switching?**\n   *Model Answer:* Saving the CPU register/state of the currently running process into its PCB and loading the saved state of the next scheduled process—allowing multitasking on one core.\n6. **Expand PCB and list three items stored inside it.**\n   *Model Answer:* **Process Control Block**. Stores: (1) Process ID (`PID`), (2) Process State (`Ready/Running/Waiting`), and (3) Program Counter & CPU Registers / Memory pointers.\n7. **Define Virtual Memory in two lines.**\n   *Model Answer:* A memory-management technique combining RAM with disk swap space so processes see a large contiguous logical memory space exceeding physical installed RAM.\n8. **Why is CLI still preferred by server/cloud engineers over GUI?**\n   *Model Answer:* CLI consumes negligible RAM/bandwidth over SSH, runs exact repeatable batch scripts effortlessly across 1,000 servers, and accesses full flags hidden in GUIs.\n9. **Name the default modern File Systems for Windows, Linux (Ubuntu), and macOS.**\n   *Model Answer:* Windows = **`NTFS`**, Linux = **`ext4`**, macOS = **`APFS`**.\n10. **What is Thrashing in OS memory management?**\n    *Model Answer:* A state where physical RAM is so overcommitted that the OS spends more time frantically swapping pages to/from disk (**page faults**) than executing actual application instructions.\n\n---\n\n### Part B: 6 Five-Mark Questions\n\n1. **Contrast System Software and Application Software with definitions, privilege modes, examples, and a summary table.**\n   *Model Answer:* Define Kernel-mode System software (OS, device drivers, compilers/utilities) vs. User-mode Application software (`Chrome, AutoCAD, Tally`), presenting Section 1's 5-row comparison table.\n\n2. **Explain Time-Sharing OS and Distributed OS with working, advantages, disadvantages, and real-world applications.**\n   *Model Answer:* Detail millisecond **Time Quantum** preemption for multi-user interactivity (`Windows/Linux`) vs. networked **multi-node pooling** presenting one unified system (`cloud clusters`), listing pros/cons from Section 2B.\n\n3. **Draw the 5-State Process Lifecycle Diagram and explain all transitions (`New, Ready, Running, Waiting, Terminated`).**\n   *Model Answer:* Sketch Section 3A's Mermaid state machine. Explain: `New -> Ready` (admission), `Ready -> Running` (dispatcher), `Running -> Ready` (time slice/interrupt preemption), `Running -> Waiting` (I/O request), `Waiting -> Ready` (I/O done), `Running -> Terminated` (exit).\n\n4. **Explain how OS Memory Management works using Paging and Virtual Memory, mentioning advantages over raw contiguous RAM allocation.**\n   *Model Answer:* Explain chopping processes into fixed **Pages** and RAM into equal **Frames** tracked via Page Tables inside MMU. Highlights: eliminates external fragmentation, runs apps bigger than physical RAM cleanly via page swapping, and enforces strict per-process isolation.\n\n5. **Compare Command Line Interface (CLI) and Graphical User Interface (GUI) across 5 points with suitable real scenarios.**\n   *Model Answer:* Compare learning curve, speed/automation, RAM/GPU usage, remote SSH suitability, and error safety (`Bash/PowerShell` vs. `Explorer/Finder`).\n\n6. **Explain File Systems (`NTFS, ext4, APFS`), directory trees, and why Journaling matters during sudden power failures.**\n   *Model Answer:* Define how file systems track metadata, permissions, and free blocks. Explain **Journaling**: logging intended metadata edits to a circular journal *before* committing writes prevents orphaned clusters and half-deleted folders after blackout crashes.\n\n---\n\n### Part C: 4 Ten-Mark Questions\n\n1. **Define an Operating System and explain its major Objectives and Core Functions (Process Management, Memory Management, File System Management, Device/I/O Management, Security) with real-life examples.**\n   *Model Answer:* Cover Convenience + Efficiency objectives, User-space vs. Kernel-space System Calls, PCB + 5-State Process lifecycle (with Mermaid diagram), Virtual Memory Paging, File directories/permissions, Device Driver abstraction, and user authentication/isolation.\n\n2. **Compare Batch, Time-Sharing, Distributed, and Real-Time Operating Systems thoroughly (working, CPU scheduling priority, pros, cons, and engineering use cases) in prose plus a comparison matrix.**\n   *Model Answer:* Present full structured analysis and complete Section 2B comparison table contrasting offline sequential batch queues, interactive quantum slicing, distributed multi-node single-system view, and deterministic Hard vs. Soft RTOS guarantees.\n\n3. **Classify Computer Software (System: OS, Translators, Drivers, Utilities; Application: General-Purpose, Custom/Specialized) with detailed working, advantages/disadvantages, and how OS boots and runs both (`Firmware/BIOS -> OS Kernel -> App`).**\n   *Model Answer:* Expand Section 1 into a full layered cake diagram (`Hardware -> BIOS/UEFI -> OS Kernel & Drivers -> System Utilities -> Application Software`), showing clearly how pressing Power runs POST, loads the OS kernel, mounts the filesystem, and executes a user app via syscalls.\n\n4. **Compare Windows, Linux, and macOS as desktop and server Operating Systems across Kernel Architecture, Default File Systems (`NTFS, ext4, APFS`), User Interfaces, Software Licensing, Security, and Suitability for B.Tech Engineering Tasks.**\n   *Model Answer:* Present Section 4B's comparison table plus detailed paragraphs comparing `NTFS/Registry/Windows`, `ext4/Open-Source/Docker/Servers` on Linux, and `APFS/Unix-certified/Silicon` on macOS, noting CLI vs. GUI roles on each platform."
